Phase 4: the draw list, and the adapter as interpreter

The contract change the whole track was for. The adapter took offsets and one
picture; it now carries out a list of instructions and decides nothing.

What the list says per frame: how long it lasts, and which part is drawn,
turned how far, with its pivot landing where. Plain data, so an animation can
be worked out and checked before any editor is involved. Instructions come in
drawing order, parents first.

The validator refuses and names the frame and the instruction rather than
skipping what it cannot draw. A frame missing a limb is a fault that gets
noticed several tracks away from its cause.

Pixels cross in bulk through the image's raw bytes. That rests on an
assumption about the application's own layout, so a runtime test reads the
same image both ways in RGB, grayscale and indexed and fails if they disagree.
The round trip is exact in all three.

The regression proof: the demonstration was rendered from the phase 3
checkpoint in a separate worktree and from the rebuilt engine, and all ten
frames are byte for byte identical. The old single-image motion is now the
degenerate case of the new machinery - one part, pushed, never turned - rather
than a second path that could drift away from it.

Those frames are committed as reference fixtures and a check compares against
them, since that equality is the only thing separating 'the machinery changed'
from 'the animation changed'. The check was proven able to fail by moving the
dash eight pixels, and it reported the seven frames that differed.

Gates: 202 unit tests, 71 runtime checks, the animation matching all ten
references, linter and formatter clean across 51 files, core layer clean, core
coverage 100 percent across 11 files.
